CHANGELOG.AB -- changes to UniPlus+ System V V.1.5+ on the Apple Lisa
September 2026

This file lists what has been ported, written or changed to bring the
network kernel (unix.net) up on the Lisa, with its programs, and what was
changed in the LisaEm emulator to run it.  It describes each change; the
code and full details are in the source tree (/usr/src) and in the git
history of the uniplus and lisaem projects.

Each entry has a "Period correct" line:

  YES      what 4.1c/4.2BSD or UniSoft had by about 1985, or a port of it
  BUG FIX  period code corrected so that it works
  PARTLY   period behaviour plus some later conveniences (explained)
  NO       a modern addition, kept as a test or convenience tool
  N/A      runs on the Mac or in the emulator, not on the Lisa

"Status" says how far each change has been tried: tested on the Lisa,
built on the Lisa, checked on the Mac only, or not yet built.


========================================================================
1. KERNEL (/usr/src/sys, /usr/src/include)
========================================================================

Network kernel headers reconstructed
  Period correct: YES
  The kernel source survived without its network headers.  All 34
  include/net headers came from UniSoft's own UniPlus+ on a Torch Triple X
  disk of the same generation (Oct 1984); sys/config.h, sys/al_ioctl.h and
  sys/speaker.h were reconstructed from the kernel, the Lisa binaries and
  the Lisa manual.  PROVENANCE.md labels every file.
  Status: tested on the Lisa (unix.net builds and boots).

Kernel build fixes
  Period correct: BUG FIX
  config.h gained NTE, so the Tecmar serial card tables link; cxstub.c
  supplies empty context routines (the real ones are for another MMU);
  the Makefile defines m68000 for the Torch IP headers; a diagnostic
  unix.pad target was added.
  Status: tested on the Lisa.

pro.c: partition e on a 20 MB ProFile
  Period correct: NO (ProFiles stopped at 10 MB)
  The second half of a 20 MB disk image is /dev/p0e (19456 blocks), the
  source filesystem mounted on /usr/src.
  Status: tested on the Lisa.

conf.c: Ethernet address 10.0.2.15
  Period correct: YES (a configuration value)
  The EtherBox address was Berkeley's 89.0.41.8; it is now the address
  LisaEm's slirp network gives the Lisa.
  Status: tested on the Lisa.

Raw ICMP sockets
  Period correct: BUG FIX
  No program had used raw sockets on this kernel.  Five faults: a raw
  ICMP socket matched the ICMP input entry and called a null pointer
  (proto.c); the raw socket code expected addresses in mbufs where this
  kernel passes pointers (raw_cb.c, raw_usrreq.c); it overwrote its own
  mbuf headers and panicked with "mfreep" (raw_cb.c, raw_usrreq.c,
  raw_ip.c); icmp_input freed echo replies twice, and read the ICMP header
  past the first mbuf (ip_icmp.c).
  Status: tested on the Lisa (ping 127.0.0.1, 10.0.2.2, 8.8.8.8).

Default routes
  Period correct: YES (as 4.2BSD)
  rtalloc() only found host and class-based network routes.  It now falls
  back to a route to 0.0.0.0, so "route add default 10.0.2.2" reaches the
  internet (route.c).
  Status: tested on the Lisa.

Setting the Ethernet address while running
  Period correct: YES (as 4.2BSD's SIOCSIFADDR)
  UniSoft's SIOCCIADDR only stored the address for the next boot.  It now
  replaces the interface's address and network route at once and
  announces the address with ARP (socket.c).  Used by ifconfig.
  Status: built on the Lisa; not yet tested.

EtherBox driver: statistics and a status check
  Period correct: BUG FIX / YES
  The driver now counts collisions (from the box's collision counter) and
  output errors (16 collisions, unreadable transmit status), as 4.2BSD
  drivers did.  A misplaced parenthesis made the "unreadable transmit
  status" check test the underflow bit instead (if_eb.c).
  Status: built on the Lisa.

Console: a fuller VT100 emulator
  Period correct: PARTLY
  vt100.c was reworked into one state machine.  VT100 behaviour added:
  tab stops every 8 columns at power-on; complete parsing of escape
  sequences, so unknown ones no longer print characters; scrolling
  regions and origin mode; save and restore cursor (ESC 7, ESC 8); index,
  next line, reverse index (now blanking the new top line); insert and
  erase characters, insert mode (VT102); autowrap on/off; application
  cursor keys (kb.c, s_kb.c); replies to "what terminal" and "where is
  the cursor"; ESC c reset; cursor hide/show (VT220, 1983).
  Later conveniences, not period: deferred wrap on the last column and
  reverse wrap for backspace (so erasing a wrapped line works), as in
  xterm; ESC [ s and ESC [ u.
  reinit.c restores the default tab stops.  A new termcap entry for vtl
  is in /usr/src/console/termcap.vtl, and is installed in /etc/termcap.
  Status: running on the Lisa; checked on the Mac (35 checks).


========================================================================
2. NETWORK LIBRARY AND DATA FILES (/usr/src/netlib)
========================================================================

Socket headers and system call stubs
  Period correct: YES
  include/net/socket.h, in.h and route.h describe the 4.1a BSD socket
  interface this kernel has (socket with a local address and options,
  accept connecting the listening socket, send/receive); sockcall.s holds
  the trap stubs for system calls 70-79.  Also include/bsd.h, mapping BSD
  names (u_long, bcopy, bzero, index) onto System V.
  Status: tested on the Lisa.

libnetdb.a: host, network, service and protocol names
  Period correct: YES (4.1c BSD library from 2.9BSD, plus 4.2BSD's
  inet_ntoa)
  gethostbyname, getservbyname and the rest, reading /etc/hosts,
  /etc/networks, /etc/protocols and /etc/services, and the inet_ address
  routines rewritten for 68000 byte order.  Names are shortened for the
  7-character limit of cc (gethbyname and so on, through netdb.h).  No
  name server: only /etc/hosts.  hosttest checks every lookup.
  Status: built on the Lisa; hosttest checked on the Mac.

/etc/hosts, networks, protocols, services
  Period correct: YES
  Sample files for LisaEm's network: localhost, lisa (10.0.2.15),
  gateway/mac (10.0.2.2), nameserver (10.0.2.3); services include telnet,
  ftp, tftp, finger and http.
  Status: installed on the Lisa.


========================================================================
3. PROGRAMS
========================================================================

tcpconn, tcpecho, looptest, udptest, netinfo
  Period correct: NO (new test programs, written for the period API)
  A TCP client and echo server, loopback TCP and UDP round-trip tests, and
  a program showing the host name and address.
  Status: tested on the Lisa.

ping
  Period correct: YES (new code; ping itself dates from December 1983)
  ICMP echo on a raw socket, run as root.  Counts late replies with their
  real times, marks duplicates, -w sets the wait, -d shows kernel ICMP
  messages.  Refuses to run on a kernel without the raw socket fixes.
  Status: tested on the Lisa.

route
  Period correct: YES (4.1a BSD route semantics)
  route show lists the kernel's routing tables; route add and route
  delete take an address or "default" and a gateway.
  Status: tested on the Lisa.

ifconfig
  Period correct: YES (new code; ifconfig is a 4.2BSD command)
  Lists interfaces with flags, addresses and packet counts; ifconfig eb0
  a.b.c.d sets the Ethernet address (needs the socket.c change).
  Status: built on the Lisa.

nc
  Period correct: NO (netcat is from 1995)
  Connect to or listen on a TCP port, or send and receive UDP; kept as a
  test tool.
  Status: not yet tested on the Lisa.

httpd
  Period correct: NO (the web is from the early 1990s)
  Serves .html and .txt files from /usr/www with HTTP/1.0; kept as a
  demonstration.
  Status: not yet tested on the Lisa.

telnet
  Period correct: YES (port of 4.1c BSD telnet from 2.9BSD)
  The kernel's select() cannot wait on a terminal, so a connection runs as
  two processes: one reads the network, one the keyboard and the escape
  commands.  Terminal modes use termio.  No "z" (no job control), and
  close does not half-close (no shutdown()).
  Status: tested on the Lisa (logging in to its own telnetd); checked on
  the Mac against a scripted server.

tftp
  Period correct: YES, with BUG FIXES
  Port of 4.1c BSD tftp.  Fixed so it works with any server: it follows
  the port the server replies from, binary mode sends "octet" (4.1c sent
  "octect"), and request packets no longer carry stray bytes.
  Status: built on the Lisa; checked on the Mac against a test server.

ftp
  Period correct: YES, with one addition (NO)
  Port of 4.1c BSD ftp with 2.9BSD's 4.1a socket conversion, 4.1c's
  XPWD/XMKD/XRMD commands and its original ruserpass (.netrc and
  environment passwords).  Bug fix: a byte of 0377 no longer ends a binary
  transfer.  Addition, not period: the "passive" command and -p, needed
  to get through LisaEm's network address translation.
  Status: built on the Lisa; checked on the Mac against an FTP server.

telnetd
  Period correct: YES (port of 4.1c BSD telnetd)
  Logins over telnet on the kernel's pseudo-terminals (mkptys makes
  /dev/ptyp0-f and /dev/ttyp0-f).  System V's login needs a utmp entry, so
  telnetd writes one first and marks it dead at logout.  mkptys first
  used ":" comments, which the shell rejected; they are "#" comments now.
  Status: tested on the Lisa (telnet 127.0.0.1 logs in; who lists the
  session).

netstat
  Period correct: YES (4.1c BSD netstat, with 4.2BSD's -s)
  Connections, interfaces (-i), routing tables (-r), mbuf counts (-m) and
  IP/TCP/UDP statistics (-s), built against the kernel's own headers.
  Status: built and run on the Lisa.

ls7 and rm7 (/usr/src/ucb)
  Period correct: YES (4.2BSD ls 1982, 4.1BSD rm 1981, from 2.9BSD)
  Berkeley ls beside System V's: columns on a terminal, -F, -A, -R, owner
  and group in -l.  It is the ls7 that root's 1984 .cshrc already names
  ("alias ls ls7 -F").  Berkeley rm takes its options as separate
  arguments.  A small opendir/readdir reads System V directories.
  Status: tested on the Lisa.


========================================================================
4. TOOLS ON THE MAC (not on the Lisa)
========================================================================

  Period correct: N/A

  extract_profile_image.py  copies files out of Lisa and Torch disk images
  make_profile_image.py     builds a ProFile image from folders
  put_profile_files.py      adds files to a disk image in place, checking
                            the filesystem before and after (--mkdir makes
                            directories); how sources reach the Lisa
  lisa_names.py             finds names that clash in 7 characters and
                            calls missing from the Lisa's libc
  vt100test                 runs the console emulator against a model of
                            the screen


========================================================================
5. LISAEM EMULATOR (not on the Lisa)
========================================================================

  Period correct: N/A

Faithful ProFile drive and parallel port emulation (lisaem #55)
  The ProFile and the parallel card's 6522 behave as the hardware does,
  replacing patches that only worked for UniPlus 1.4's kernel, so rebuilt
  kernels boot.  Also fixed a Timer 1 latch fault that crashed UniPlus
  after a power cycle.

EtherBox emulation with slirp networking (lisaem #57)
  Emulates the 3Com EtherBox on a dual parallel card port, and connects it
  to the Mac and the internet through libslirp: the Lisa is 10.0.2.15,
  the Mac 10.0.2.2.  Port forwards reach the Lisa from the Mac; packet
  traces and pcap files for debugging.

slirp TFTP server (lisaem etherbox-tftp)
  LISAEM_ETHERBOX_TFTP=directory lets the Lisa fetch files from the Mac
  with tftp (read only, binary mode).

Double bus fault halts instead of quitting; mouse crash (lisaem #58)
  UniPlus's reboot command made LisaEm quit.  A 68000 double bus fault now
  halts the CPU and powers the Lisa off, as the real CPU halts; a crash
  when the mouse moved during a message box is fixed.


========================================================================
6. KNOWN ISSUES
========================================================================

  - reboot still ends in a double bus fault: the kernel jumps to the ROM
    monitor with an unusable stack.  Use sync and power off instead.
  - The EtherBox's lost-interrupt problem (3Com memo, May 1983) is not
    handled by the driver; LisaEm's timing hides it.
  - root's 1984 .cshrc aliases rm to "rm -i", which breaks "rm -f"; use
    "alias rm rm7 -i" with ls7/rm7 installed, or remove the alias.
